Bound the controller-ready wait so a contended device cannot spin forever.

_wait_controller_ready() loops until the controller answers STATUS_READY and counts
failures only inside `except TypeError`. That branch is unreachable: read_packet()
swallows USBError and returns None, and the `bool(resp) and` guard -- added so resp[0]
would not raise TypeError on None -- means nothing raises at all. So errcount never
increments, the `if errcount > 50: quit(-99)` escape never fires, and the loop runs
without end.

Measured on an Alienware 14: 25 concurrent invocations each spun here for over ten
minutes in R state, hammering get_status after having issued _reset("all-lights-on") --
which made the keyboard flash and meant no colour ever landed.

This counts every attempt that did not come back ready, which is what the existing
bound of 50 was always meant to do.

--- a/alienfx/core/controller.py
+++ b/alienfx/core/controller.py
@@ -153,10 +153,13 @@
             pkt = self.cmd_packet.make_cmd_get_status()
             logging.debug("SENDING: {}".format(self.pkt_to_string(pkt)))
             self._driver.write_packet(pkt)
+            resp = None
             try:
                 resp = self._driver.read_packet()
-                ready = bool(resp) and (resp[0] == self.cmd_packet.STATUS_READY)
             except TypeError:
+                pass
+            ready = bool(resp) and (resp[0] == self.cmd_packet.STATUS_READY)
+            if not ready:
                 errcount += 1
                 logging.debug("No Status received yet... Failed tries=" + str(errcount))
             if errcount > 50:
